@startuml uart-pin-order
' Why every reset put one 0xFF on the TX line, and the two-line fix.
' Grounded in Src/board.c (board_init) and the logs of 2026-09-22.

title One 0xFF per reset: the order of two register writes

skinparam shadowing false

concise "PA2 function" as F
robust "TX line (PA2)" as TX

@0
F is "analog (reset)"
TX is high

@10
F is "AF0 (not the UART)"
TX is low

@14
F is "AF7 = USART2_TX"
TX is high

@0 <-> @10 : reset, then board_init starts
@10 <-> @14 : MODER written, AFRL not yet

note top of TX
  BEFORE: MODER = alternate function first, AFRL = AF7 second.
  For the few instructions in between the pin is on AF0, not the
  UART, and the line drops. A short low pulse followed by high is
  read by the ST-Link as a start bit plus eight ones: 0xFF.
  Measured: 46 resets, 46 bytes, all 0xFF.
  The low pulse is inferred from the byte, not seen on a scope.
  That the ORDER causes it is proven by the before/after test.
end note

legend bottom
  AFTER: AFRL = AF7 first, MODER = alternate function second, as
  ST's HAL_GPIO_Init does. The pin enters alternate function mode
  already connected to the UART, whose idle level is high, so the
  line never drops. Measured: 0 bytes in at least 6 resets, with
  nothing else changed between the two tests.
  Verified in the flashed image, not only in the source: the stores
  to 0x48000020 (AFRL) come before those to 0x48000000 (MODER).
endlegend

@enduml
